{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2\n\nFive questions on §4 and §5 — one API frozen, one API recommended, and the difference that word makes.\n\n**1.** ⚪ State both halves of what the Kubernetes project"}},"session_id":"3394a1de-4e66-45cd-9a2d-6f9704f5f018","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"eae66ab7-3615-48c2-a3f8-d0c402381ab7"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" has said about the Ingress API.\n\n**2.** ⚪ True or false, with justification: *Ingress is deprecated and will be removed in a future release.*\n\n**3.** 🟡 Name the three role-mapped Gateway API resources"}},"session_id":"3394a1de-4e66-45cd-9a2d-6f9704f5f018","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"4c6d18b8-2d0f-4fd6-b2f3-00b246373fb6"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" and say which organisational role each belongs to.\n\n**4.** 🟡 How many GatewayClasses is a Gateway associated with, and how many Routes can attach to one Gateway?\n\n**5.** 🟡 A request arrives at a Gate"}},"session_id":"3394a1de-4e66-45cd-9a2d-6f9704f5f018","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"994817ca-22de-40c9-971f-469e880076eb"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"way's IP address. Name the header the reverse proxy uses to match a configuration, and name the two optional things the HTTPRoute may do before the request is forwarded.\n\n---\n\n**Answers with Explanations:**\n\n**1. It is frozen:"}},"session_id":"3394a1de-4e66-45cd-9a2d-6f9704f5f018","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"d8817ed5-c66a-476a-a4e0-51c50d25df46"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" generally available and subject to the stability guarantees for GA APIs, with no plans for removal — *and* no longer being developed, with no further changes or updates** [source: k8s-docs-ingress-depth-2026-08-24].\n\nAn answer carrying only one half is wrong, because"}},"session_id":"3394a1de-4e66-45cd-9a2d-6f9704f5f018","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"c80a080d-a5dd-45d7-9a5c-518f0a582340"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" one half is precisely the error being tested.\n\nAnd it is worth saying *why* both halves matter rather than just repeating them. The stability half means there is **no migration emergency**: what you are running keeps working, keeps being supported, and is"}},"session_id":"3394a1de-4e66-45cd-9a2d-6f9704f5f018","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"516779cb-5f6c-4a8b-9343-0466c82beba1"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" not scheduled for removal. The no-development half means there is **no future capability**: whatever gap you find in Ingress today is permanent. Those two facts drive different decisions, one about existing systems, one about new ones, and that"}},"session_id":"3394a1de-4e66-45cd-9a2d-6f9704f5f018","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"21e6041b-f64d-46f5-ac3c-5d92d9b70024"}
